# Regression test: a `terminate`d job must be reported `Terminated` by `jobs` exactly
# once, even when two `jobs` listings are taken back-to-back with no settle delay in
# between -- not "Terminated" on both.
#
# Root cause (two, actually, both closed by the same fix): `terminate` kills an
# external `cmd` job's child with a real signal. `cmd.rs`'s own waiter thread
# (`spawn_child_wait`) then separately observes that signal death and calls
# `GlobalState::mark_terminated_by_signal` -- which used to unconditionally take a
# *second* `terminated_snapshot`, resurrecting one a first `jobs` listing may have
# already consumed and reported. Even after guarding that re-snapshot (skip it if the
# job's status already reads `Terminated`), a *second*, more fundamental race remained:
# once the one-time snapshot is consumed, a later listing falls through to the job's
# live data, which can still legitimately read `Terminated` for a little while after
# the snapshot was consumed (the underlying thread hasn't finished exiting and being
# reaped yet) -- so an immediate second listing could still show the job a second time,
# just via the live branch instead of a resurrected snapshot.
#
# Fixed by `JobData::terminated_reported` (see `src/lang/state/global_state.rs`): set
# the very first time `jobs_for_listing` ever reports a job `Terminated`, via *either*
# path, and checked by every later call, which skips that job entirely once it's set --
# regardless of whether its live data is still alive. This is the one place the
# existing, sleep-500ms-delayed sibling tests (`terminated_job_is_reaped.crush`,
# `cmd_paused_then_terminated_is_reaped.crush`) don't actually exercise: with a 500ms
# gap before the first listing, the job's underlying thread had already finished and
# been reaped by then in practice, so their "first listing" always read from the
# snapshot branch, never raced the live branch at all.
#
# Each listing is a bare `jobs | materialize` -- deliberately *not* `jobs | where
# {...} | materialize` (the pattern the sibling tests use): the extra `where` pipeline
# stage's own setup overhead was enough, in practice, to incidentally give the killed
# job's underlying thread time to finish and get reaped between the two listings,
# masking the exact race this test exists to catch. Confirmed directly: with `where`
# added back in, this test still passed even with the `terminated_reported` fix
# disabled (only the narrower, insufficient-by-itself re-snapshot guard in place).
# Filtering by the distinctive sleep duration happens *after* both listings are
# already captured, on the already-materialized tables, instead.
$job_id := $(cmd sleep 8172 &)
sleep $(duration:of milliseconds=300)
terminate $job_id

# No settle delay between these two listings -- that's the entire point: catching the
# job mid-death, not safely after it's already fully gone.
$first_all := $(jobs | materialize)
$second_all := $(jobs | materialize)

$first_rows := $($first_all | where {$definition:starts_with "\$cmd \"sleep\" 8172"} | materialize)
$second_rows := $($second_all | where {$definition:starts_with "\$cmd \"sleep\" 8172"} | materialize)

$first_count := $($first_rows | count)
$second_count := $($second_rows | count)
assert ($first_count == 1) "a terminated job should be reported once on the first jobs listing after terminate"
$first_status := ($first_rows[0]):status
assert ($first_status == "Terminated") "the one-time report of a terminated job should have status Terminated"
assert ($second_count == 0) "an immediate second jobs listing, with no settle delay, must not report the same terminated job again"
echo "done"
